Relicensing history
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

This project began as a fork of https://github.com/TzurSoffer/SpotifyStatsTracker,
which is published under the MIT License. This fork was itself distributed under
the MIT License through commit 3be61d7, the last state of version 1.45.0 before
the relicensing. That grant is irrevocable: anyone who obtained the code at or
before that commit keeps MIT rights to it permanently, and nothing here
withdraws them. The AGPL-3.0-or-later terms above apply from the relicensing
commit onward.

Portions of this codebase used to remain the copyright of Tzur Soffer under
the MIT License, with its notice preserved in a LICENSE.MIT file. Phase 2 of
the dependency rewrite (2026-07-30) replaced the last of those portions: the
spotipyFree wrapper was superseded by the project's own Database/Spotify/
client, the pre-SQLite migrators were deleted behind a version floor, and the
remaining upstream-attributed lines in the application code, templates and
documentation were rewritten. As of the commit series concluding with the one
that introduced this paragraph, `git blame` attributes no current line to the
upstream author, tests/test_license_headers.py enforces that this stays so,
and LICENSE.MIT was removed - there is no longer any MIT-licensed portion in
this tree whose notice it would preserve. The upstream project's own MIT
licensing of ITS tree, and the irrevocable MIT grant described above for
historical states of this fork, are of course unaffected.

The upstream project cannot merge changes from this fork back into its
MIT-licensed tree without a separate grant from the copyright holder of those
changes.


-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Third-party components
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The source tree in this repository references its Python dependencies rather
than bundling them. Built artifacts - notably the Docker image produced by the
Dockerfile - do bundle them, and conveying such an artifact conveys those
components too. The one exception is static/js/vendor/, which is checked in:
the Content-Security-Policy in config.py allows scripts from 'self' only, so a
browser dependency has to be served from this origin rather than a CDN, and
this project ships no JS build step that could fetch one at package time.

The components whose licenses carry source-availability obligations are listed
here, followed by the vendored browser components.

htmx
  License:  Zero-Clause BSD (see static/js/vendor/htmx.LICENSE)
  Upstream: https://github.com/bigskysoftware/htmx
  Version:  2.0.9
  Asset:    https://github.com/bigskysoftware/htmx/releases/download/v2.0.9/htmx.min.js
  sha256:   57d9191515339922bd1356d7b2d80b1ee3b29f1b3a2c65a078bb8b2e8fd9ae5f

  Vendored at static/js/vendor/htmx.min.js, byte-for-byte as published - the
  hash above is checkable against the release asset, and .gitattributes keeps
  git from rewriting the bytes. Pinned to a stable 2.x release deliberately:
  the repository's own "latest release" is a 4.0.0 beta, which is not what a
  live instance should be served.

  0BSD imposes no notice-retention or attribution requirement at all, so
  nothing here is obligatory; it is recorded because a checked-in binary whose
  provenance is undocumented is the thing worth avoiding, not a license risk.

  Version bumps are deliberate, with any fallout fixed in the same commit -
  same reasoning as the pinned spotapi commit above and the pinned ruff/eslint
  versions in the lint workflow. Update the version, asset URL and hash here
  when it moves.

